"} "Round-robin pools advance teams to single-elimination tournament for first four places."]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The tournament rules were slightly changed for the 1972 Olympic football tournament, as the quarter-finals were replaced by a second round of round-robin play. Another rule stipulated that should one of the final matches end in a tie after extra time, both teams would receive gold or bronze medals. This rule had a negative effect on play, as the Soviet Union and East Germany were not willing to risk losing their bronze medal in the extra time of their match. The public booed them as they finished the match without attacking."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The final was played between Poland and Hungary, the 1964 and 1968 champions. Poland was coached by " [:a {:href "/athletes/1200716"} "Kazimierz Górski"] ", who also managed the Polish national team. With future stars like " [:a {:href "/athletes/26956"} "Grzegorz Lato"] ", the Poles were considered to be the best team of the tournament. In the final, they trailed Hungary at half-time, but two goals by " [:a {:href "/athletes/26925"} "Kazimierz Deyna"] " turned the match around. Two years later, Górski led the Polish team to a third place at the World Cup - also in West Germany - with many of the players that took the gold medal in München."] "\n"] [:button {:id "button753004", :class "btn btn-default"} "Details"] [:script {} "$('#button753004').click(splits)"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {}] [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Team"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "Goals"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "Yellow Cards"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "Red Cards"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "Penalty Kicks"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "Shots"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "Shots on Goal"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "Corner Kick